  ],
  "campus": "Atlanta",
  "campus_description": "ATL@ATLANTA",
  "course_description": "Instruction in rhetorical composition, critical reading, reflection, and writing as recursive process. Across themed sections, students engage with diverse genres. Courses share some common assignments including research writing and final student-curated portfolio with reflection project.",
  "course_notes": "(Freshmen Only) Drums of Liberation - How does sound shape and make a story? Can we determine anything about ourselves, our societies, or our histories from the ways stories are sonically told? If so, how might we translate these examinations into text? In this course, we will critically engage sound and music in film, television, social media, and literature to consider how the sonic informs our lives amidst a sight-centered world. Through emphasizing Black, queer, and feminist methodological approaches to listening across media, we will also learn to apply sound and music to how we construct and present arguments about the content we consume. This class will feature work by writers including but not limited to James Baldwin, Toni Morrison, Ryan Coogler, Nina Simone, Nikki Giovanni, and Alice Smith as well as works of pop culture from anime and Disney films to paintings and plays. Students will leave the course with the tools and skills necessary to listen for underlying arguments and construct their own in ways that build on existing conversations.",
  "requisites": null,
  "cross_listed": null,
